1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,686
Your phone can find

2
00:00:01,686 --> 00:00:03,000
your cat in your photos

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,658
yet nobody ever gave it rules

4
00:00:04,658 --> 00:00:06,144
for what a cat looks like.

5
00:00:06,494 --> 00:00:08,063
So how does it do it?

6
00:00:08,063 --> 00:00:10,603
It saw tons of examples and worked

7
00:00:10,603 --> 00:00:12,395
out the patterns itself.

8
00:00:12,395 --> 00:00:13,740
That's today's AI.

9
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:24,429
A traditional program works differently:

10
00:00:24,429 --> 00:00:26,378
people write the rules one by one

11
00:00:26,378 --> 00:00:28,149
and the computer follows them.

12
00:00:28,499 --> 00:00:30,465
For payroll or booking flights

13
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:32,955
the rules are clear, so that's enough.

14
00:00:33,305 --> 00:00:34,995
But some things are hard

15
00:00:34,995 --> 00:00:36,263
to write rules for

16
00:00:36,263 --> 00:00:38,587
like reading a handwritten three.

17
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:39,926
Fat, skinny, tilted

18
00:00:39,926 --> 00:00:41,475
you know it instantly.

19
00:00:41,825 --> 00:00:43,623
Now try writing down how.

20
00:00:43,623 --> 00:00:45,133
"Two curves stacked"?

21
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:46,212
So is an eight.

22
00:00:46,212 --> 00:00:48,010
"Bottom half opens left"?

23
00:00:48,010 --> 00:00:49,089
So does a five.

24
00:00:49,439 --> 00:00:51,365
Rules like that miss some threes

25
00:00:51,365 --> 00:00:53,532
and mistake other digits for threes.

26
00:00:55,033 --> 00:00:58,035
So most of today's AI flips it around:

27
00:00:58,035 --> 00:00:59,931
no rules, just examples.

28
00:01:00,281 --> 00:01:01,922
Show the computer hundreds

29
00:01:01,922 --> 00:01:04,069
or thousands of handwritten digits

30
00:01:04,069 --> 00:01:05,395
each with its answer:

31
00:01:05,395 --> 00:01:07,478
this is a three, this is a seven.

32
00:01:07,828 --> 00:01:09,511
It finds the patterns itself

33
00:01:09,511 --> 00:01:11,615
and that's called machine learning.

34
00:01:11,965 --> 00:01:14,052
What it learns is called a model.

35
00:01:14,052 --> 00:01:15,696
Show the model a new image

36
00:01:15,696 --> 00:01:17,404
and it gives you an answer.

37
00:01:17,754 --> 00:01:19,843
A lot of people think programmers

38
00:01:19,843 --> 00:01:21,742
coded in everything AI can do.

39
00:01:21,742 --> 00:01:23,768
Mostly, it learned from examples

40
00:01:23,768 --> 00:01:24,971
which we call data.

41
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,830
Let's actually try it

42
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:33,797
with nearly 1,800 handwritten digits

43
00:01:33,797 --> 00:01:35,527
each a tiny 8×8 grid.

44
00:01:35,877 --> 00:01:38,307
First we lock away 300 as the test

45
00:01:38,307 --> 00:01:41,165
and the rest are examples to learn from.

46
00:01:41,515 --> 00:01:43,687
Learning from them is called training

47
00:01:43,687 --> 00:01:45,331
and during training it never

48
00:01:45,331 --> 00:01:46,564
sees a test question.

49
00:01:46,914 --> 00:01:48,901
Only questions it hasn't seen can

50
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:50,646
show whether it truly learned

51
00:01:50,646 --> 00:01:51,730
or just memorized.

52
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:53,226
So, take a guess:

53
00:01:53,226 --> 00:01:54,642
with just 10 examples

54
00:01:54,642 --> 00:01:57,271
how much of the test does it get right?

55
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:00,286
Pick ten at random

56
00:02:00,286 --> 00:02:01,644
and often some digits

57
00:02:01,644 --> 00:02:03,003
don't show up at all.

58
00:02:03,353 --> 00:02:05,093
We tried twenty different

59
00:02:05,093 --> 00:02:06,415
random sets of ten.

60
00:02:06,415 --> 00:02:08,642
The typical score was about 37%:

61
00:02:08,642 --> 00:02:11,287
better than the 10% you'd get guessing

62
00:02:11,287 --> 00:02:12,261
but still low.

63
00:02:12,611 --> 00:02:16,205
With a hundred examples, it gets 88%.

64
00:02:16,205 --> 00:02:19,508
With all 1,500 or so, it gets 97%.

65
00:02:19,858 --> 00:02:21,924
We never gave it rules for a three

66
00:02:21,924 --> 00:02:24,113
just told it which ones were threes.

67
00:02:24,113 --> 00:02:25,207
In this experiment

68
00:02:25,207 --> 00:02:27,273
more examples meant better scores.

69
00:02:27,623 --> 00:02:29,700
Still, it missed 10 of the 300

70
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:32,192
like this messy two it called a one.

71
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:38,140
Here's another way to see it:

72
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:40,360
today's AI is a prediction machine.

73
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:41,946
Not predicting the future

74
00:02:41,946 --> 00:02:43,278
just seeing something

75
00:02:43,278 --> 00:02:44,800
and guessing the answer.

76
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:48,042
Our model also says how sure it is.

77
00:02:48,042 --> 00:02:50,768
It's 96% sure this one's a three.

78
00:02:51,118 --> 00:02:52,832
Other AI works the same way.

79
00:02:52,832 --> 00:02:55,098
Give a language model half a sentence

80
00:02:55,098 --> 00:02:56,812
and it guesses the next word

81
00:02:56,812 --> 00:02:57,608
then the next

82
00:02:57,608 --> 00:02:59,384
until there's a whole answer.

83
00:02:59,734 --> 00:03:01,366
Chatbots built on large

84
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:03,565
language models reply this way.

85
00:03:03,915 --> 00:03:04,903
They guess well

86
00:03:04,903 --> 00:03:06,418
but a convincing answer

87
00:03:06,418 --> 00:03:07,670
can still be wrong.

88
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,995
So how do all these terms fit together?

89
00:03:11,995 --> 00:03:14,504
Picture AI, artificial intelligence

90
00:03:14,504 --> 00:03:16,081
as the biggest circle:

91
00:03:16,081 --> 00:03:17,658
computers doing things

92
00:03:17,658 --> 00:03:19,450
that seem to take smarts.

93
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:21,244
A chess program built

94
00:03:21,244 --> 00:03:23,650
on hand-written rules counts as AI.

95
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:25,850
A plain payroll program doesn't.

96
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,076
Inside AI is machine learning

97
00:03:28,076 --> 00:03:30,017
and inside that, deep learning

98
00:03:30,017 --> 00:03:31,957
which runs on neural networks:

99
00:03:31,957 --> 00:03:33,704
many layers of simple math.

100
00:03:34,054 --> 00:03:35,943
"Deep" just means many layers

101
00:03:35,943 --> 00:03:37,442
not deep understanding.

102
00:03:37,442 --> 00:03:38,810
Large language models

103
00:03:38,810 --> 00:03:40,699
are built with deep learning.

104
00:03:41,049 --> 00:03:42,578
When I say "today's AI,"

105
00:03:42,578 --> 00:03:44,107
I mean machine learning:

106
00:03:44,107 --> 00:03:45,955
AI that learns from examples.

107
00:03:50,067 --> 00:03:52,648
Learning from data can go wrong, too.

108
00:03:52,648 --> 00:03:54,043
According to Reuters

109
00:03:54,043 --> 00:03:55,647
Amazon once tried using

110
00:03:55,647 --> 00:03:57,112
AI to screen résumés.

111
00:03:57,462 --> 00:03:58,962
It learned from 10 years

112
00:03:58,962 --> 00:04:00,837
of résumés sent to the company

113
00:04:00,837 --> 00:04:01,712
mostly by men.

114
00:04:02,062 --> 00:04:04,339
In effect, it taught itself that male

115
00:04:04,339 --> 00:04:05,754
candidates were better.

116
00:04:06,104 --> 00:04:08,280
Résumés with the word "women's,"

117
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:10,659
as in "women's chess club captain,"

118
00:04:10,659 --> 00:04:11,747
got marked down.

119
00:04:12,097 --> 00:04:14,016
The tool was later scrapped.

120
00:04:14,016 --> 00:04:15,455
If the data is biased

121
00:04:15,455 --> 00:04:17,579
the model can pick that up too.

122
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:24,150
When you use AI, remember three things.

123
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:26,220
First, it may answer anyway

124
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:28,386
even about things it's never seen.

125
00:04:28,386 --> 00:04:30,488
Show our digit model the letter A

126
00:04:30,488 --> 00:04:32,017
and it still has to pick

127
00:04:32,017 --> 00:04:33,164
from zero to nine.

128
00:04:33,514 --> 00:04:35,999
It says it's 92% sure that's a four.

129
00:04:35,999 --> 00:04:38,830
But it can only pick from the ten digits.

130
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:40,243
A kid would say

131
00:04:40,243 --> 00:04:43,006
"That's not a number." The model won't.

132
00:04:43,356 --> 00:04:45,577
Second, it gives you predictions

133
00:04:45,577 --> 00:04:46,548
not the truth.

134
00:04:46,548 --> 00:04:48,214
Remember that messy two?

135
00:04:48,214 --> 00:04:50,642
The model was 81% sure it was a one

136
00:04:50,642 --> 00:04:51,822
and it was wrong.

137
00:04:52,172 --> 00:04:54,396
Third, what it gets right or wrong

138
00:04:54,396 --> 00:04:55,639
depends on its data

139
00:04:55,639 --> 00:04:58,387
and also on how it's designed and trained.

140
00:04:58,737 --> 00:05:00,875
So to judge whether an AI is reliable

141
00:05:00,875 --> 00:05:03,245
first find out what data it learned from.

142
00:05:03,595 --> 00:05:06,733
Whenever an AI sounds totally sure, ask yourself:

143
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:09,231
has it seen questions like this before?

144
00:05:10,733 --> 00:05:12,205
Most of today's AI isn't

145
00:05:12,205 --> 00:05:13,922
built on rules people write.

146
00:05:13,922 --> 00:05:15,945
It learns patterns from examples.

147
00:05:16,295 --> 00:05:18,497
Next time, we'll look at why a model can

148
00:05:18,497 --> 00:05:19,928
ace the practice questions

149
00:05:19,928 --> 00:05:21,579
and still stumble on new ones.

150
00:05:21,579 --> 00:05:23,506
That's often a sign of overfitting.
